## MVP scope (implement now)

Build the minimal pipeline defined in `docs/design/mvp.md`:

1. Lex → parse → resolve → type-check → lower → emit bytecode
2. Load and **verify** bytecode, then execute on a **single-process stack VM**
3. Enforce required entrypoint: `main :: () => { ... }`

**MVP in scope:** numeric primitives, `bool`, tuples, unit, raw pointers, fixed arrays, slices, structs, enums, pattern matching, `Option<T>`, `Result<T, E>`, `?`, control flow, arithmetic/comparison/logical operators.

**MVP out of scope — do not implement yet:**

- M:N scheduler, actors, mailboxes, supervision
- Full borrow checker / ownership verifier (beyond use-after-move)
- Primitive `string` type — MVP is byte-first (`u8`, arrays, slices, pointers, allocation intrinsics)
- Std I/O, networking, rich collections
- JIT, hot reload, `#derive` codegen

When adding a language feature, follow this order:

**lexer → parser → AST node → resolver → type checker → IR lowering → codegen → bytecode verifier → VM → tests**

Do not skip resolver for multi-file or `#import` work. Do not build post-MVP runtime modules until MVP passes its acceptance checklist.

---

## Project architecture

The Phoenix compiler and VM are divided into distinct pipeline stages. Each stage is its own module and owns its data structures. Never reach across stages.

```
src/
  lexer/        — tokenization; source text → token stream
  parser/       — parsing; token stream → AST
  ast/          — AST node definitions, visitor/fold traits
  resolver/     — name resolution, `#import`, symbol tables, scoping
  typeck/       — type checking and inference; Result/Option rules, use-after-move
  lower/        — AST → IR lowering
  ir/           — intermediate representation definitions
  codegen/      — IR → bytecode emission
  bytecode/     — file format, serialization, loader, verifier
  vm/           — MVP: stack interpreter; post-MVP: scheduler, actors
  runtime/      — post-MVP: actor lifecycle, mailboxes, supervision
  std/          — Phoenix standard library implemented in Rust
  utils/        — arena allocator, interning, diagnostics
  error/        — compiler error types, diagnostics, span reporting
```

One primary concern per file. Never put lexer logic in the parser. Never put type checking in codegen.

